---
/**
 * ParticleEffects.astro — DISABLED by default
 *
 * Reasoning:
 * Even with throttling, mouse trail + click burst particles were causing
 * measurable CPU load on mid-range laptops and high battery drain on mobile.
 *
 * The fx layer is still rendered (zero size, zero cost) so existing
 * `window.burstParticles()` calls don't crash. But no DOM particles are
 * spawned anymore.
 *
 * To re-enable opt-in (e.g., on high-end desktops only), flip ENABLE_PARTICLES
 * to true below — but consider that particles provided minimal UX value
 * relative to their CPU cost.
 */
---

<div id="fxLayer" class="fx-layer" transition:persist style="display:none"></div>

<style>
  .fx-layer {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 9998;
    overflow: hidden;
    display: none; /* never rendered visually */
  }
</style>

<script>
  // No-op stub — keeps `window.burstParticles` calls from throwing
  window.burstParticles = function () { /* particles disabled for perf */ };
</script>
